          "param": "k",
          "low": 2,
          "high": 4
        ,
          "choices": null
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.5
    ,
      "why": "unrolled_k is my fallback if iterative's Fmax is too low to reach 1 MSPS: k chained micro-rotations/cycle raises throughput at the cost of a wider combinational datapath (more area). I keep k modest (2-4) because the spec is area-first, so I only want enough unrolling to clear the throughput constraint, not to chase throughput. Same accuracy-focused data_width/n_iter/guard ranges as iterative so the two fronts are directly comparable and the merged Pareto front covers the area-vs-accuracy trade-off."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "No fleet facts yet, so I start from first principles on the two families I own. The spec is area-first with a hard accuracy bound (max_abs_err <= 2^-10, i.e. accuracy_bits >= 10) and a hard throughput bound (>= 1 MSPS). Accuracy depends only on data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding, so I bound those tightly around the 10-bit accuracy target: data_width 12-16 and n_iter 10-16 give enough headroom to clear 2^-10 without oversizing the datapath (which would inflate area for no accuracy benefit, since the accuracy_bits reference is 10). angle_guard -2..1 and frac_guard 0..2 let Optuna trade a little accuracy margin against area, and both rounding modes are kept. For the throughput constraint, iterative is the cheapest area but its Fmax is uncertain; unrolled_k with small k (2-4) is the area-minimal way to guarantee >=1 MSPS if iterative falls short. I split the budget 50/50 between the two so the merged front captures the area/accuracy/throughput trade-off and the final min-area selection has a feasible candidate from whichever family clears the constraints. If iterative proves infeasible on throughput, the unrolled_k front still covers the spec; if unrolled_k is too area-heavy, iterative wins on the min-area selection rule."
}

